"A CI/CD pipeline diagram shows automated jobs and gates that transform a source change into a deployable or deployed result.\n\nUse when: Review delivery dependencies and required checks. Explain how failures or approvals affect release progression.\n\nAvoid when: The only question is where one immutable artifact has been promoted.\n\nVisual grammar: Stages or job nodes identify build, validation, packaging, and deployment behavior. Dependency edges determine eligibility to run. Artifact and gate markers distinguish outputs from decisions or required evidence.\n\nBuild: Choose a workflow trigger and define its successful outcome. Lay out job prerequisites with parallel checks in separate lanes. Add artifacts, environment gates, and failure outcomes. Keep approval labels outside arrows and pad stage groups for readable headers.\n\nCheck: A deployment path cannot bypass its stated gates. Run status is distinguished from workflow design. Artifacts identify their originating revision or digest. Parallel job edges and failure paths never cover stage text.\n\nPitfalls: Treating a green build as proof of successful production deployment. Drawing a required approval that is not enforced by the actual workflow.\n\nExample: A main-branch change runs checks, builds an immutable package, then deploys only after the configured environment gate.\n\nAlternatives: Artifact promotion diagram, Software supply-chain provenance graph."
